Yes that never landed but it should not matter. That path anyway only handles SetWheelMode responses which are not happening at all if Solaar or other user space tool is not used. As we saw from the debug logs in the first test. And even if some SetWheelMode was not handled, then it would only mean the driver would work exactly as before.

What browser are you using? 

One more test I can think of is to produce evtest logs in controlled manner with failing and succeeding driver module.

1. Make sure all user space tools that can send HID++ traffic are not running (libratbagd, piper, Solaar, OpenRGB)
2. Make sure wheel is in ratchet (clicky) mode.

3. Checkout f0866517be93 and compile the module
4. Load the module
   sudo rmmod hid_logitech_hidpp
   sudo insmod hid-logitech-hidpp.ko
5. Unplug and plug the receiver
6. Move your mouse and wait few secs to make sure it is awake
7. Start event recording with: sudo evtest
8. Run the test:
   - Scroll 10 single clicks DOWN. One click at a time, pausing couple of seconds after each click.
   - Scroll 10 single clicks UP. One click at a time, pausing couple of seconds after each click.
9. Make ~5 seconds of continuous scrolling in one direction without changing direction.
10. Stop evtest recording and store to a file

Then do same steps 3-10 again by checking out f0866517be93 and  reverting commit f0866517be93. If you have time you could do the test again with both commit f0866517be93 and then commit f0866517be93 reverted to have 4 test run in total with logs.

Make sure to use same WEB page for scrolling each time. For each test, note whether scrolling felt unusually fast or not.

Then send each of the log files to me, and I can try to analyze if I see some difference in event generation between the driver versions.
</pre>
